course: dictionary
lesson: 7930
title: Real: true, actual, or just strong?
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Real is a small word with many jobs, so context tells you which one it has. It can mean something that exists and is not imagined: «Dragons are not real.»
explain: It can also mean true or not fake, as in «real gold», or actual, as in «the real reason». Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence.
explain: Real can also make another word stronger, as in «a real friend». Here it does not change the meaning much; it just says the friend is a true, proper friend. In casual talk, «real quick» means very quick.
fact: Real means existing in fact, not imagined.
fact: Real can mean true or not fake, as in «real gold».
fact: «The real reason» means the actual, true reason.
fact: In maths, real numbers are all the numbers on the number line.
fact: Casual talk: «for real?» asks if someone is serious, and «real quick» means very quick (informal, US).
exercise: «Dragons are not real.» What does real mean here? | Made of gold | *Existing in fact, not imagined | Very big and loud
exercise: In «the real reason», which word could replace real? | Imaginary | *Actual | Silly | Pretend
exercise: Ella says, «I'll be there real quick.» What does real quick mean? | *Very quick | Quick and real | Quick to see | Not quick at all
test: «A blurry or faraway photo is not real proof.» What does real proof mean? | Made up | *True and can be trusted | Blurry and far away | Old and worn
test: In maths, real numbers are all the numbers on which thing? | *Number line | Dice | Clock face | Map
exam: «Online, people may not be who they say they are, so not everything is real.» What does real mean in this sentence? | *True and not fake | Imaginary | Happening right now | Lovely and kind
